# The bytes GitHub Pages serves must match what a clone produces.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHY IT LOOKS LIKE TIDYING
#
# `core.autocrlf` is on for this machine. Committing terms.html on 2026-09-11
# produced git's own warning:
#
#     in the working copy of 'terms.html', LF will be replaced by CRLF the
#     next time Git touches it
#
# All four files are LF in the index today, but nothing was pinning them - so a
# fresh clone here would check out CRLF, and the first commit made from that
# copy would write CRLF into the index and change every byte GitHub Pages
# serves. Silently, and on a page whose URL is registered in the Meta app
# settings.
#
# The sibling repository heyjaanu-site already carries this exact file, added
# after the same warning, with a note calling it load-bearing. This one did not,
# which is the only reason the lesson had to be learned twice.
*.html text eol=lf
*.txt  text eol=lf
*.xml  text eol=lf
*.png  binary
